1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,624
Aarav opens a broken earphone mic.

2
00:00:02,624 --> 00:00:04,750
Inside sits a tiny round capsule.

3
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:08,589
Its signal is so weak, it
can barely push any current.

4
00:00:08,889 --> 00:00:12,518
Yet a part inside reads it,
without taking current from it.

5
00:00:12,818 --> 00:00:13,718
How?

6
00:00:13,309 --> 00:00:15,828
That part is a field effect transistor.

7
00:00:15,828 --> 00:00:16,732
An FET.

8
00:00:17,232 --> 00:00:20,398
Think of a soft garden hose
with water running through it.

9
00:00:20,748 --> 00:00:22,894
Squeeze the hose with your hand.

10
00:00:22,894 --> 00:00:24,390
Less water comes out.

11
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:27,068
Squeeze hard enough, and the flow stops.

12
00:00:27,418 --> 00:00:29,471
Your hand takes no water at all.

13
00:00:29,471 --> 00:00:31,425
It only presses from the sides.

14
00:00:32,075 --> 00:00:34,139
An FET works the same way.

15
00:00:34,139 --> 00:00:36,231
Its hose is called the channel.

16
00:00:36,581 --> 00:00:40,253
Current flows through the channel,
from the source to the drain.

17
00:00:40,603 --> 00:00:42,719
The gate is the squeezing hand.

18
00:00:42,719 --> 00:00:44,335
It sits on both sides.

19
00:00:44,685 --> 00:00:47,518
A voltage on the gate
squeezes the channel.

20
00:00:47,518 --> 00:00:49,486
Almost no gate current flows.

21
00:00:50,136 --> 00:00:54,336
Let's look inside a junction FET, or JFET.

22
00:00:54,686 --> 00:00:57,190
Start with a bar of n type silicon.

23
00:00:57,190 --> 00:00:58,694
This is the channel.

24
00:00:59,044 --> 00:01:02,268
N type means it has extra free
electrons to carry current.

25
00:01:02,618 --> 00:01:04,062
One end is the source.

26
00:01:04,062 --> 00:01:05,358
Electrons enter here.

27
00:01:05,708 --> 00:01:07,338
The other end is the drain.

28
00:01:07,338 --> 00:01:08,569
Electrons leave here.

29
00:01:08,919 --> 00:01:11,844
Now add two p type
regions, one on each side.

30
00:01:12,194 --> 00:01:14,168
They are joined together inside.

31
00:01:14,168 --> 00:01:15,683
This pair is the gate.

32
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:18,881
Where p meets n, we get p n junctions.

33
00:01:18,881 --> 00:01:20,953
So it is a junction FET.

34
00:01:21,603 --> 00:01:24,114
In the symbol, the gate arrow points in.

35
00:01:24,114 --> 00:01:25,481
That means n channel.

36
00:01:25,831 --> 00:01:28,753
In a p channel JFET, everything flips.

37
00:01:28,753 --> 00:01:30,252
The arrow points out.

38
00:01:30,902 --> 00:01:32,980
Now let's run it on the breadboard.

39
00:01:33,330 --> 00:01:36,879
A supply, VDD, pushes
electrons from source to drain.

40
00:01:37,229 --> 00:01:39,786
A second supply makes the gate negative.

41
00:01:39,786 --> 00:01:41,261
This is reverse bias.

42
00:01:41,611 --> 00:01:44,579
At zero gate voltage,
the channel is wide open.

43
00:01:44,579 --> 00:01:45,980
Ten milliamperes flow.

44
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:48,626
Now make the gate minus one volt.

45
00:01:48,626 --> 00:01:50,151
The grey layers grow.

46
00:01:50,501 --> 00:01:52,095
They are depletion layers.

47
00:01:52,095 --> 00:01:53,832
Zones with no free carriers.

48
00:01:53,832 --> 00:01:55,061
They block current.

49
00:01:55,411 --> 00:01:57,023
The channel gets narrower.

50
00:01:57,023 --> 00:01:58,546
The drain current drops.

51
00:01:58,896 --> 00:02:00,090
Minus two volts.

52
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:01,177
Narrower still.

53
00:02:01,177 --> 00:02:02,974
Watch the scope step down.

54
00:02:03,324 --> 00:02:06,119
At minus four volts, the two layers touch.

55
00:02:06,119 --> 00:02:07,637
The channel is closed.

56
00:02:07,987 --> 00:02:09,909
The drain current is zero.

57
00:02:09,909 --> 00:02:12,705
This gate voltage is called VGS off.

58
00:02:13,355 --> 00:02:16,183
And the gate current stayed
near zero the whole time.

59
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:19,100
Let's write the working in small steps.

60
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:20,350
One.

61
00:02:19,951 --> 00:02:22,317
The gate junction is reverse biased.

62
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:24,437
So gate current is almost zero.

63
00:02:25,137 --> 00:02:26,037
Two.

64
00:02:25,528 --> 00:02:28,203
A more negative gate makes
the depletion layers wider.

65
00:02:28,903 --> 00:02:29,803
Three.

66
00:02:29,477 --> 00:02:31,889
A narrower channel has more resistance.

67
00:02:31,889 --> 00:02:33,500
R equals rho L over A.

68
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:35,100
Four.

69
00:02:34,679 --> 00:02:36,955
More resistance means less drain current.

70
00:02:37,655 --> 00:02:38,555
Five.

71
00:02:38,277 --> 00:02:41,432
At VGS off, the channel pinches shut.

72
00:02:41,432 --> 00:02:42,642
ID is zero.

73
00:02:43,342 --> 00:02:45,398
Only electrons carry this current.

74
00:02:45,398 --> 00:02:47,107
So an FET is unipolar.

75
00:02:47,457 --> 00:02:50,098
ABJT needs both electrons and holes.

76
00:02:50,098 --> 00:02:51,390
So it is bipolar.

77
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,522
ABJT is worked by current.

78
00:02:54,522 --> 00:02:57,061
An FET is worked by voltage.

79
00:02:57,861 --> 00:02:58,808
Worked example.

80
00:02:58,808 --> 00:03:01,428
How hard is it to push
current into the gate?

81
00:03:01,778 --> 00:03:03,863
The gate is at minus two volts.

82
00:03:04,213 --> 00:03:06,656
A tiny leakage current
flows into the gate.

83
00:03:06,656 --> 00:03:07,869
Just one nanoampere.

84
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:10,428
A nanoampere is one
billionth of an ampere.

85
00:03:10,778 --> 00:03:15,059
Input resistance is the gate
voltage divided by the gate current.

86
00:03:15,759 --> 00:03:18,692
That is two volts over ten
to the minus nine amperes.

87
00:03:19,392 --> 00:03:21,652
Two times ten to the nine ohms.

88
00:03:21,652 --> 00:03:23,321
Two thousand mega ohms.

89
00:03:24,021 --> 00:03:26,941
So the gate takes almost
nothing from the signal.

90
00:03:26,941 --> 00:03:28,473
Perfect for a weak mic.

91
00:03:29,123 --> 00:03:30,586
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:03:30,936 --> 00:03:34,662
Many students forward bias
the gate to turn the JFET on.

93
00:03:35,012 --> 00:03:35,912
Wrong.

94
00:03:35,550 --> 00:03:38,586
The gate to source junction
is always reverse biased.

95
00:03:39,236 --> 00:03:41,900
Forward bias lets gate current flow.

96
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,453
The huge input resistance is lost.

97
00:03:44,803 --> 00:03:46,427
Where is this asked?

98
00:03:46,427 --> 00:03:48,291
In PUC board papers.

99
00:03:48,941 --> 00:03:53,607
Draw the construction, explain the
working, and compare a JFET with

100
00:03:53,607 --> 00:03:54,512
a BJT.

101
00:03:55,162 --> 00:03:56,062
Try this.

102
00:03:56,037 --> 00:04:00,264
An n channel JFET has VGS
off of minus four volts.

103
00:04:00,264 --> 00:04:03,840
The gate goes from minus
one to minus three volts.

104
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:04,929
What happens?

105
00:04:10,329 --> 00:04:11,229
B.

106
00:04:10,747 --> 00:04:14,509
The depletion layers widen,
and the drain current falls.

107
00:04:14,859 --> 00:04:19,178
Minus three volts is more
reverse bias, so the layers grow.

108
00:04:19,528 --> 00:04:23,921
The channel narrows, its
resistance rises, and ID falls.

109
00:04:24,271 --> 00:04:26,798
But minus three is not yet minus four.

110
00:04:26,798 --> 00:04:28,682
So the current is not zero.

111
00:04:29,332 --> 00:04:30,878
Screenshot this card.

112
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:36,116
Next, JFET characteristics.

113
00:04:36,116 --> 00:04:39,324
What do the curves and
r d, g m and mu tell us?
